  "query_preview": "..\n(Saturday night through Wednesday)\nIssued at 923 PM CDT Wed Jul 22 2026\n\nA ridge dominated upper level pattern will prevail across much of \nthe Lower-48 as we head into next week. Forecasts of 500mb heights \nlate Sat indicated strong upper ridging sprawled from the Desert SW, \nsouthern Rockies, and the High Plains from Wyoming to the Big Bend. A\ndeep upper low over the Hudson Bay was producing troughing which\nextended southward along the East Coast. This firmly places the \ngreater Tennessee Valley in a NW flow aloft, which appears will \ncontinue into the middle of next week. A number of passing upper \nlevel disturbance within this stream will help contribute to \nconvection impacting the area early in the period. Exact timing and \nplacement of them is rather difficult, so am depending upon ensemble\nand blend guidance versus deterministic output. \n\nAt the surface, a frontal boundary now moving in a NW-SE manner\nacross the area should return as a warm front late Fri into Sat. This\nwill bring additional lower level moisture from the Gulf of America\nfurther inland. Output from the NBM has precipitable water amounts of \nover 2\" across the forecast from Sat evening into Sun morning. With\npassing upper level support, there will be good chances for numerous\nshowers and thunderstorms to impact the Tennessee Valley. Some of the\nstorms could become strong. Of greater worry is rainfall output in\nsuch a humid environment. Excessive rainfall and a potential of  \nflooding cannot be ruled out; which will be dependent upon how much \nrainfall occurs beforehand. The frontal boundary per DESI and NBM\nguidance should move southward again late Sun into Mon, with shower\nactivity ending for the start of a new work week. \n\nDaily temperatures into the middle of next week appear will be on the\nrise, in part thanks to the strong upper ridge just to our west. As\nshower activity ends on Sun, the heat will return. Highs to start a\nnew week should range from the mid/upper 80s to the lower 90s west. \nEven warmer conditions are expected for the Mon-Wed timeframe, with \nhighs all three days mainly in the low/mid 90s, and lows in the\nlow/mid 70s. Heat index values on Sun should range from the mid/upper\n90s east to 107 in the more urbanized spots. Another need for Heat \nAdvisories maybe needed Sun, but appear more likely next Mon/Tue, \nwith values ranging from the upper 90s to 109 in the hottest spots. \nThey could be some relief by the midweek, as another frontal boundary\nmoves across the area from the north. This boundary will also bring \nlower end rain chances back to parts of the Valley.",
